ction matrix to its reduction and then to K, so the reduced action on b_i is x b_i=(lambda-i+1)b_(i-1), y b_i=(i+1)b_(i+1), h b_i=(lambda-2i)b_i (out-of-range terms zero). This is the explicit representation correspondence; the characteristic-zero e_i themselves are not being used as vectors in M tensor_Z K.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/3/objects\\\",\\\"value\\\":[\\\"a\\\",\\\"e_i\\\",\\\"x\\\",\\\"y\\\",\\\"h\\\",\\\"lambda\\\",\\\"M_R\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"F_p\\\",\\\"K\\\",\\\"M\\\",\\\"L(K)\\\",\\\"b_i\\\"]},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/4/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"For 0<=r<=lambda<p, r! is invertible modulo p, so the reductions of X_r and Y_r are scalar multiples of the corresponding powers of x and y on M tensor_Z K. The characteristic-zero identities defining E_i,j preserve M_R and their matrices in the R-basis a e_t are the usual matrix-unit matrices; therefore their reductions, followed by scalar extension to K, are the matrix units in the explicitly defined basis b_0,...,b_lambda. Equivalently, the identities from S002 persist under these maps; every coefficient binom(lambda,j) is nonzero modulo p (since 0<=j<=lambda<p), hence a unit in F_p and K. Thus any K-subspace W invariant under x and y is invariant under each reduced E_i,j. Write a nonzero w in W as w=sum_i c_i b_i and choose i with c_i nonzero. For every j, the reduced E_j,i sends w to c_i b_j, so b_j belongs to W because c_i is invertible in K. Hence W is the whole module. Its basis has lambda+1 vectors, a positive integer, so it is nonzero. This proves irreducibility, including lambda=0 and p=2.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/4/objects\\\",\\\"value\\\":[\\\"r\\\",\\\"lambda\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"X_r\\\",\\\"Y_r\\\",\\\"x\\\",\\\"y\\\",\\\"E_i,j\\\",\\\"K\\\",\\\"W\\\",\\\"b_i\\\"]}]}\" }","status":"completed"}}

